  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name then family name. Czech surnames change for women: most add -ová (Novák, Nováková; Dvořák, Dvořáková) and adjective names switch -ý to -á (Novotný, Novotná; Černý, Černá); the lists give the male form. Since 2022 a woman may choose to drop the -ová. Name days (svátek) are still in every calendar and people get flowers on theirs. Short forms are everywhere: Honza for Jan, Pepa for Josef, Kuba for Jakub, Verča for Veronika, Káťa for Kateřina, Míša for Michal or Michaela. Slovak names follow the same pattern. Vietnamese Czechs put the family name first in Vietnamese (Nguyễn Thị Lan) and many also go by a Czech name in shops and at school. Ukrainians and Russians use their passport spelling; their -ov or -skyi names usually become -ova or -ska for women.",
